                    },
                    "message": "Issue #1: Missing Redfish API State Verification After GUI Toggle\n\nLocation: Line 240 in gui/gui_test/security_and_access_menu/test_policies_sub_menu.robot\n\nIssue: Test toggles GUI but doesn't explicitly verify the Redfish API state changed to False before testing authentication. While Toggle Policy Option And Verify Toast Message verifies GUI state, it doesn't confirm the backend API property ${resp[\"Oem\"][\"OpenBMC\"][\"AuthMethods\"][\"BasicAuth\"]} actually changed to False.\n\nFix: Add explicit Redfish API verification after GUI toggle:\n# After line 237, add:\n```\n${resp_after}=  Redfish.Get Properties  ${REDFISH_ACCOUNTS_SERVICE_URI}\nShould Be Equal  ${resp_after[\"Oem\"][\"OpenBMC\"][\"AuthMethods\"][\"BasicAuth\"]}  ${False}\n...  msg=BasicAuth API state did not change after GUI toggle\n```\n\nWhy: Ensures GUI changes propagate to backend API, preventing false positives if GUI toggle succeeds but API state doesn't change"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gui/gui_test/security_and_access_menu/test_policies_sub_menu.robot",
                    "line": 237,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Sridevi Ramesh",
                        "email": "sridevra@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SrideviRamesh"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gui/gui_test/security_and_access_menu/test_policies_sub_menu.robot",
                    "line": 422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "George Keishing",
                        "email": "gkeishin@gmail.com",
                        "username": "gkeishin"
                    },
                    "message": "Incorrect Variable Comparison - Variable in Quotes\n```\n# Current code (test_policies_sub_menu.robot:422)\nIF  '$initial_option_value' == 'Enabled'\n\n```\n# Previous code (patchset 2, line 396)\n\n```\nIF  '${initial_option_value}' == 'Enabled'\n```\nFix Required:\n```\nIF  ${initial_option_value} == 'Enabled'\n```\n\nWhy: BOTH versions are incorrect. When a variable is enclosed in quotes in Robot Framework, it's treated as a literal string, not a variable reference. Both '${initial_option_value}' and '$initial_option_value' compare the LITERAL STRING against 'Enabled', not the variable's value. The variable must be outside the quotes to be evaluated. This bug exists in both patchset 2 and 3, but patchset 3's change makes it more obvious. The test likely never worked correctly."
                },
                {
                    "file": "gui/gui_test/security_and_access_menu/test_policies_sub_menu.robot",
                    "line": 422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Sridevi Ramesh",
                        "email": "sridevra@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SrideviRamesh"
                    },
                    "message": "As discussed, this fails\n\n```\nVariables in the original expression \"${initial_option_value} == 'Enabled'\" were resolved before the expression was evaluated. Try using \"$initial_option_value == 'Enabled'\" syntax to avoid that. See Evaluating Expressions appendix in Robot Framework User Guide for more details.\n-----------\n```\nHence keeping the changes as is"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gui/gui_test/security_and_access_menu/test_policies_sub_menu.robot",
                    "line": 422,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Sridevi Ramesh",
                        "email": "sridevra@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "SrideviRamesh"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
